Welcome to notebook three. The previous notebook gave you values, the things your program holds onto. This notebook gives you the verbs that act on them. Functions and methods are the unit of behaviour in Scala.

Scala has two related but distinct ideas. There are methods, defined with the keyword def, which are attached to an enclosing class, object, or scope. And there are functions, which are first class values you can pass around like integers. Understanding the small gap between methods and functions, and how Scala bridges it for you automatically, is the single most important skill for reading idiomatic code. This matters especially for Spark, because Spark's entire application programming interface is built on passing functions to operators like map and filter.

Let us start with the method. The keyword def introduces a method definition. The shape is, def, then the name of the method, then a parameter list inside parentheses with each parameter typed, then a colon and the return type, then an equals sign, then the body expression. The body is a single expression. Its value is what the method returns. There is no return keyword needed. The last expression in the body is the result.

For example, a method called square that takes one Int parameter named x and returns an Int is written as, def square, open paren, x colon Int, close paren, colon Int equals, x times x. Calling square of seven returns forty nine. A method called greet that takes a String parameter and returns a String is written, def greet, open paren, name colon String, close paren, colon String equals, then on the next line, indented, the interpolated string hello comma space dollar sign name.

When the body is longer than one line, it becomes a block expression. The same rules as in notebook two apply. The block's value is the value of its last expression, and that is what the method returns. If you want to compute a hypotenuse, you can bind the two squared sides as local values, then call the math square root method on their sum. The local values go out of scope when the method returns. The square root is what the method evaluates to.

Now an important rule. Scala will infer the type of a val. Scala will not infer the types of method parameters. Every parameter must carry a type annotation. If you forget one, the compiler stops you. Why this rule? A method is a contract. Callers need to know what they can pass in. Inferring parameter types at the call site would make signatures fragile and hard to read. So parameter types are mandatory. Return types are optional, with a caveat we will get to in a moment.

Method parameters can have default values. If the caller omits an argument, the default is used. For example, a greet method that takes a name and an optional greeting parameter with the default value hello can be called in three different ways. You can pass just the name, in which case the greeting defaults to hello. You can pass both name and greeting positionally. Or you can pass arguments by name, like name equals ganesh, greeting equals namaste. When you pass by name, the order does not matter. You can write greeting first and name second if you want.

Defaults let you grow an application programming interface without breaking existing callers. Named arguments make calls self documenting. They are especially valuable when several parameters share the same type, for example when a method takes three Ints in a row. With positional arguments at the call site you cannot tell which Int is which. With named arguments you can.

Now, return types. Scala can infer a method's return type from its body. But the inferred type leaks the implementation. The same guidance from notebook two applies, just sharper. For public methods, write the return type. It documents the contract. A change to the body that subtly changes the type will fail loudly at the boundary, not silently inside callers. For local or private helpers, let inference do its job. There is one case where the return type is not optional but required. Recursive methods. A method defined in terms of itself, like factorial, must declare its return type, because the compiler cannot infer the type of something that references itself.

What about methods that return nothing useful? Those return Unit. A Unit returning method is called for its side effect. Printing to the console, mutating something, writing to a file. It returns the single Unit value, written as a pair of empty parentheses, which carries no information. The body of a Unit returning method is just a sequence of statements ending in something that does not produce a value. There is a deprecated short form where you omit the equals sign, like def announce paren name colon String close paren, brace, body, brace. Avoid that form in Scala 3. The explicit colon Unit equals form is clearer about intent. This method exists for its side effect, not its value.

Now the second concept. Functions are values. Methods live attached to a scope. Function values, also called lambdas or anonymous functions, are objects you can name, store, return, and pass to other functions. The syntax to write a function value is a parameter list in parentheses, an arrow that looks like an equals sign followed by a greater than sign, and a body expression. For example, val double equals open paren, x colon Int, close paren, arrow, x times two. Calling double of seven returns fourteen. Another example, val add equals open paren, a colon Int comma b colon Int, close paren, arrow, a plus b. Calling add of three and four returns seven.

The type of a function value is written with the same arrow between the parameter list and the result type. The double function has type Int arrow Int, which you read as, takes an Int and returns an Int. The add function has type, open paren Int comma Int close paren, arrow, Int. You read that as, takes an Int and an Int and returns an Int. That arrow notation appears everywhere in Scala. Get fluent with reading it.

Once you have function values, you unlock higher order functions. A higher order function is one that takes a function as an argument, returns a function as a result, or does both. This is the bread and butter of functional style Scala and of Spark's entire application programming interface. For example, a method called applyTwice that takes a function f from Int to Int and an Int x can apply that function twice. Its body is f of f of x. Calling applyTwice of double and five computes double of double of five, which is twenty.

When you pass a lambda inline as an argument, Scala has a useful shorthand. The underscore. You can write nums dot map of underscore plus one, and Scala expands that to nums dot map of, x arrow x plus one. The underscore stands for a parameter that is used exactly once, in order. It is idiomatic for short lambdas like incrementing or comparing. Use the full x arrow form when the body is non trivial. The underscore form is great when the lambda is one tight expression.

Functions can also be returned from other functions. Consider a method called adder that takes an Int n and returns a function from Int to Int. The body of adder is the function value, x arrow x plus n. Calling adder of five returns a new function. That returned function adds five to whatever you give it. You can name it, call it plusFive, and then plusFive of ten gives you fifteen. The returned function carries the value five with it. That captured value is what closures are made of, and we will come back to closures in a minute.

Now the precise distinction between methods and functions. A method, defined with def, is attached to a scope. It is not a value by itself. A function, written with the arrow syntax, is a value of some function type. You can store it in a val. You can put it in a list. You can pass it as an argument. Methods are not first class values. Functions are.

So how do you pass a method where a function value is expected? Scala's answer is called eta expansion. The compiler automatically converts a method into a function value when the context expects one. If you have a method called square and you call nums dot map of square, the compiler quietly rewrites that as nums dot map of, x arrow square of x. In Scala 3 this conversion happens automatically in any context that expects a function type. You do not have to do anything. If you ever need to force the conversion explicitly, you can append a trailing underscore to the method name, like square underscore. You will rarely need that. The practical takeaway is straightforward. Write your behaviour as methods using def. Define them once. Let the compiler hand them to map, filter, and flatMap as function values when needed.

Now a useful variation. A Scala method can have more than one parameter list. The body can use parameters from all of them. For example, def add open paren a colon Int close paren, open paren b colon Int close paren, colon Int equals, a plus b. Calling add of three and then of four returns seven. Two payoffs come from this shape.

The first payoff is partial application. You can call the method with just the first list and you get back a function ready to be called with the rest. For example, val addThree equals add of three. The expression add of three does not produce a number. It produces a new function of type Int arrow Int. That function adds three to whatever you give it. Calling addThree of ten returns thirteen.

The second payoff is that type inference flows left to right across parameter lists. A type the compiler infers from the first list is known by the time it reads the second list. We will lean on this idea hard in notebook eleven, when we meet given and using clauses. Spark itself uses multi parameter lists in some operators where the first list specifies what to do and a later list specifies how to do it, in a builder style API.

Now, closures. A closure is a function value that captures references to variables in the surrounding scope at the moment the function value is created. Recall the adder example. When you call adder of ten, the returned function value remembers ten. When you call adder of one hundred separately, that second returned function remembers one hundred. Each call to adder returns a different function value, because each one closes over a different n. The captured value travels with the function. That is the cleanest way to package a small piece of state with the behaviour that uses it.

Closures matter enormously in Spark. When you write rdd dot map of, x arrow x plus offset, Spark serialises that closure, including the captured offset value, and ships it to every executor in the cluster. The executors run the function with their own copy of offset. If offset were instead a mutable variable that lives only on the driver, the executors would see a stale snapshot, not the live value. Notebook fifteen will revisit closures from the serialisation angle, because that is where many real Spark bugs come from.

Let us close this notebook with a small example that uses every piece. First, define a method called isAbove. It takes a threshold in its first parameter list and a value in its second parameter list, and it returns a Boolean. Second, partially apply it to produce a function value with the threshold baked in. Call the result isAboveTen. Its type is, Int arrow Boolean. Third, use the higher order method filter on a list of numbers, passing isAboveTen as the predicate. The result is the sublist of numbers greater than ten. And fourth, write the same idea inline with an underscore lambda. Both lines compile to the same bytecode. The choice between them is purely a style question. Extract a named function when the predicate has a meaningful name, is complex, or is used more than once. Inline a lambda when it is small and used once.

You can now write methods, define functions as values, pass them to higher order operators, and capture surrounding state in closures. Notebook four introduces collections. List, Vector, Map, Set, and Array. Those are the data shapes that all the higher order functions you just learned will operate on. After that, notebook five ties everything together with map, filter, fold, and for yield over real data.
